# cython: language_level=3, binding=True, annotation_typing=False, embedsignature=False
# cython: cdivision=True
"""
WGPU backend implementation based on Dawn.

Dawn (https://dawn.googlesource.com/dawn) is the WebGPU implementation used by
Chromium. It ships a shared library with the standard ``webgpu.h`` C API.

This module is written in Cython: the classes below are regular Python classes
(subclassing the public API in ``wgpu._classes``), but their methods are
compiled to C and call into Dawn directly. The C declarations of the complete
API (``_webgpu.pxd``) are generated by the codegen from Dawn's ``webgpu.h``,
so every struct field and function used here is checked at compile time.

Each object holds its native pointer in a small extension type (``_Handle``),
so that hot-path methods like ``draw()`` or ``set_bind_group()`` reach the C
pointer without any Python attribute lookups.

The structure of this module deliberately mirrors that of the wgpu-native
backend (``backends/wgpu_native/_api.py``).

The same module also runs in Pyodide (``sys.platform == "emscripten"``). It is
then compiled against Emdawnwebgpu, Dawn's implementation of ``webgpu.h`` on top
of the browser's WebGPU (see ``tools/build_dawn.py``). The differences are
small, and marked with ``_IS_EMSCRIPTEN``:

* Callbacks are called by the browser's event loop (``AllowSpontaneous``)
  instead of by ``wgpuInstanceProcessEvents()``.
* The browser cannot block, so ``sync_wait()`` suspends the Python stack with
  JSPI (``pyodide.ffi.run_sync``), which requires that the code is run via
  e.g. ``pyodide.runPythonAsync()``. Awaiting works everywhere.
* Validation errors arrive asynchronously, so they are logged instead of
  raised at the call site.
* The surface is a ``<canvas>`` element, and the browser presents it.
"""

